What Is Pulse-Width Modulation (PWM)?

Pulse-Width Modulation (PWM) is a technique used by a microcontroller to control the amount of power delivered to a device by rapidly switching a digital signal ON and OFF.
Instead of producing a continuously changing voltage, PWM produces a series of square-wave pulses.

1. Frequency
Frequency tells us how many times the signal repeats every second.

For example:

1 kHz = 1,000 cycles per second
10 kHz = 10,000 cycles per second

The time for one complete cycle is called the period.

$$ \boxed{Period = \frac{1}{Frequency}} $$

So, for a 1 kHz PWM signal:

$$ Period = \frac{1}{1000}=0.001s=1ms $$

Therefore, each cycle lasts 1 millisecond.
